  "content": "• Volume and capacity of cones: deriving and applying the formula V = ⅓πr²h; relating to the volume of a cylinder of equal base and height\n• Volume and capacity of pyramids (square and rectangular base): deriving and applying V = ⅓ × base area × height\n• Volume and capacity of frustums of cones: applying V = ⅓πh(R² + Rr + r²) using dimensions of the original cone and the removed top cone\n• Volume and capacity of frustums of pyramids: applying V = ⅓h(A₁ + A₂ + √(A₁A₂)) for square and rectangular bases\n• Relationship between volume units and capacity units (cm³ ↔ mL, m³ ↔ L; 1 L = 1 000 cm³)\n• Reading and interpreting measurements of real containers (buckets, grain stores, water tanks, cooking pots) shaped as cones, pyramids and frustums\n• Selecting and applying appropriate formulae to solve real-life volume and capacity problems\n• Project: making and measuring model containers shaped as frustums, pyramids and cones from locally available materials",
